EXPERIMENT 56 – PERFORMANCE TESTING ON STUDENT PORTAL USING APACHE JMETER
AIM
To use Apache JMeter to execute performance testing on a student portal.
ALGORITHM
1. Start Apache JMeter and rename the Test Plan.
2. Add a Thread Group.
3. Add User Defined Variables for host and port.
4. Add HTTP Request Defaults and HTTP Cookie Manager.
5. Set:
   - Number of Threads: 20
   - Ramp-up Period: 10 seconds
   - Loop Count: 2
6. Add four HTTP Requests: Login Page, Login, Dashboard and Results.
7. Add a Duration Assertion of 3000 milliseconds.
8. Add Summary Report and Aggregate Report.
9. Run the test and analyse average response time, throughput and error percentage.
INPUT
Test Plan Configuration
Element	Property	Value
User Defined Variables	host	localhost
User Defined Variables	port	8080
HTTP Request Defaults	Protocol	http
HTTP Request Defaults	Server	${host}
HTTP Request Defaults	Port	${port}
Thread Group	Threads	20
Thread Group	Ramp-up	10 seconds
Thread Group	Loop Count	2
HTTP Request 1	GET	/login
HTTP Request 2	POST	/login
HTTP Request 3	GET	/dashboard
HTTP Request 4	GET	/results
Duration Assertion	Duration	3000 ms

Login POST parameters:
- regno = test registration number
- password = test password
OUTPUT
Aggregate Report
Label	Samples	Error %
Login Page	40	0.00%
Login	40	0.00%
Dashboard	40	0.00%
Results	40	0.00%
TOTAL	160	0.00%

Average, 90% Line, 95% Line and Throughput depend on the student portal.
RESULT
Thus, Apache JMeter was used to execute performance testing on a student portal, and the results were verified.

Note: Execute load, stress and soak tests only against your college test server or a local application copy. Do not run them against websites you do not own.
